/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  batch26_cooling_failure  --  a BATCH exothermic reaction held at T_p by
  its jacket, and what happens when the jacket stops.

  THE REACTION IS FICTITIOUS: A + B -> P in an inert solvent S, an ideal
  liquid with no vapour phase in the reactor (constant/reactions and
  constant/components/reactantA.dat say how every number was set).  It is
  a teaching witness for the process-safety EduTool, not a model of any
  real chemistry, and its records are coherent by construction: the heat
  of reaction, -60.000 kJ per mol of B, comes from formation data whose
  differences are the only thing that carries meaning, and it does not
  depend on temperature (dCp = 0).  The whole charge -- 5 mol A, 5 mol B,
  15 mol S -- is in the vessel at t = 0.  That is what makes a batch the
  WORST case for thermal safety: at t = 0 all of the reactant is
  unconverted, so a cooling failure at the start releases the whole
  adiabatic temperature rise.

  THE SCENARIO (the recipe below):

     t < t_f   isothermal at T_p = 330 K; the jacket removes the heat of
               reaction (the energy ledger records that duty)
     t = t_f   `setParameter reactor adiabatic 1` -- the jacket stops
     t > t_f   adiabatic: the B still unconverted at t_f (the
               ACCUMULATION, X_ac) reacts and heats the mass to T_cf

  The thermal-safety estimate is T_cf = T_p + X_ac * dT_ad (a batch's
  mass does not change, so Stoessel's semi-batch mass correction is 1),
  with dT_ad the adiabatic rise of the full charge from T_p (run this case
  with the failure at t = 0 to have the engine compute it).  Because
  dCp = 0 here, the total heat capacity of the charge does not change as
  it reacts, and the estimate is EXACT for this batch: the engine's T_cf
  (T_final of a failure run) and the estimate agree to the integration
  error.  The MTSR is the largest T_cf over every failure instant: for a
  batch, the failure at t = 0.

  `value 0` on the same event keeps the jacket working: the same case then
  runs the normal isothermal batch, which is how the EduTool draws X_ac(t).

  WHAT IS NOT MODELLED: the vessel has no vapour phase -- past the
  mixture's boiling point a real open vessel would boil and a closed one
  would pressurise.  The MTSR is defined on exactly that hypothetical,
  which is why it is compared with the boiling point (MTT) rather than
  trusted beyond it.  No decomposition is modelled either: T_D24 is an
  assumption the EduTool's reader sets.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        reactor;
        type        batchReactor;

        operation
        {
            mode         isothermal;       // held by the jacket until the recipe takes it away
            T_setpoint   330 K;            // T_p, the process temperature
        }

        reaction    AB_to_P;              // see constant/reactions
    }
);

recipe
(
    //  THE COOLING FAILURE.  `value 1`: from this instant the jacket removes
    //  no heat and the reactor is adiabatic.  `value 0`: the jacket keeps
    //  working (normal operation; nothing changes).
    {
        time        600;        // s   t_f, the moment the cooling fails
        action      setParameter;
        unit        reactor;
        key         adiabatic;
        value       1;
    }
);
